# Private-namespace members and their homes, one "DICT /name" per line.
# The dict-homes test holds the file in both directions: every name here
# must still be where it says in data/*.ps, and every member those
# sources define must be named here. A member may not move home, vanish,
# or arrive unregistered. A feature that adds one registers it in the
# same commit, so the file is the running register of where interpreter
# machinery lives.
#
# The "entries" line counts the member lines that follow. Nothing else
# would stop the list being emptied: with nothing registered there is
# nothing to look for, and both directions would agree over two empty
# sets.
#
# A name here is the whole name, and it ends where PostScript ends one --
# at whitespace or a delimiter. A register that let a name end anywhere
# else could carry one nothing defines and have it answered by a longer
# name that something does: /DATA by DATA_DIR, /.hodgman by
# .hodgman-sutherland. Such an entry registers a member that does not
# exist while the one that does goes unheld.
#
# A name here is also one the PostScript defines, not one a comment or a
# printed message about it spells. /.h is what data/init.ps writes in the
# line that shows an imaginary helper to explain the helper-call idiom;
# nothing defines a member of that name, and registering it would hold
# nothing to anything.
#
# More members are registered than the sources are read as defining. One
# defined in C, one a running procedure computes, and the device class
# dictionaries a loop copies into their home are written down nowhere a
# scan can read as a definition, so they are held by the first direction
# alone.
entries 415

# Graphics-state template slots (data/gstate.ps). The list is the whole
# fails the check, so a new slot is declared here in the commit that adds
# it, and none may be dropped or renamed.
# of the template: a slot the template declares and this list does not
# the device class dictionaries, one per PostScript-implemented device
